<section class="section-block">
	<h2>Before you start</h2>
	<ol>
		<li><strong>A VoiceML account with a phone number.</strong> See <a href="/voiceml/">VoiceML</a> or <a href="/signup/">start a free trial</a>.</li>
		<li><strong>A six-digit code for each person</strong>, and the number each one should be put through to.</li>
		<li><strong>The SHA-256 hash of each code.</strong> On Linux, <code>printf '%s' 427351 | sha256sum</code> prints it; on macOS, use <code>shasum -a 256</code> in place of <code>sha256sum</code>. Use <code>printf</code> rather than <code>echo</code>, which adds a newline and changes the hash.</li>
	</ol>
	<div class="alert alert-warning" role="note">
		<p><strong>The codes and numbers in these documents are samples.</strong> The hashes are for the codes 427351 and 916204, and the numbers use the fictional 555-0100 to 555-0199 range. Replace them all before you publish.</p>
	</div>
</section>

<section class="section-block">
	<h2>Step 1: Publish the verification document</h2>
	<p>Replace each hash with the hash of a real code, and each number with that person&rsquo;s line. Then publish it at <a href="{{ site.voicemlPublisherUrl }}">voiceml.org</a>. It contains Liquid tags, so it is published to an <code>/r/</code> URL. Keep the URL exactly as returned, <code>key</code> included, and don&rsquo;t post it publicly.</p>
	<p class="code-block-header">verify.xml</p>
	<pre class="code-block" data-lang="xml"><code>{{ voicemlSamples["imf-pin-line"].verify | escape }}</code></pre>
</section>

<section class="section-block">
	<h2>Step 2: Publish the main document and point the number at it</h2>
	<p>Replace <code>VERIFY_URL</code> with the <code>/r/</code> URL from step 1 and publish. Set the <code>/s/</code> URL it returns as the phone number&rsquo;s voice URL.</p>
	<p class="code-block-header">main.xml</p>
	<pre class="code-block" data-lang="xml"><code>{{ voicemlSamples["imf-pin-line"].main | escape }}</code></pre>
</section>

<section class="section-block">
	<h2>Step 3: Test it</h2>
	<ol>
		<li>Call the number and enter one of your codes. You are greeted by name and the matching phone rings.</li>
		<li>Call again and enter any other six digits. You hear &ldquo;Access denied&rdquo; and the call ends.</li>
	</ol>
</section>

<section class="section-block">
	<h2>How the check works</h2>
	<p>The <code>&lt;Gather numDigits="6"&gt;</code> sends the code to the verification document as <code>Digits</code>. There, <code>{% raw %}{% assign entered = Digits | sha256 %}{% endraw %}</code> turns it into the same 64-character hash that <code>sha256sum</code> prints, and a <code>{% raw %}{% case entered %}{% endraw %}</code> compares it with each agent&rsquo;s hash. A match sets the agent&rsquo;s name and number; <code>&lt;Number&gt;{% raw %}{{ line }}{% endraw %}&lt;/Number&gt;</code> dials whichever number was set. Anything else falls through to &ldquo;Access denied&rdquo; and <code>&lt;Hangup/&gt;</code>.</p>
	<p>Sending wrong codes to a hang-up instead of a second try is deliberate. A document can&rsquo;t count attempts or remember earlier calls, so it can&rsquo;t lock anyone out; ending the call is the only brake it has on guessing.</p>
</section>

